/**
 * Queueable job that creates a CSAT survey Task for each closed Case that
 * has a Contact. Designed to be bulk-safe (accepts a set of Case Ids rather
 * than a single Id) so it can be invoked once per DML transaction no matter
 * how many cases were closed at the same time (mass list-view close,
 * nightly API cleanup of up to 200 records, etc.).
 *
 * Marked Database.AllowsCallouts so the planned vendor survey integration
 * can be added later inside execute() without changing the trigger or the
 * enqueue pattern.
 */
public with sharing class CaseSurveyJob implements Queueable, Database.AllowsCallouts {

    private final Set<Id> caseIds;

    public CaseSurveyJob(Id caseId) {
        this(new Set<Id>{ caseId });
    }

    public CaseSurveyJob(Set<Id> caseIds) {
        this.caseIds = caseIds;
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {
        if (caseIds == null || caseIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Re-query to work on current, authoritative data at execution time
        // (the job may run some time after it was enqueued).
        List<Case> closedCases = [
            SELECT Id, CaseNumber, ContactId
            FROM Case
            WHERE Id IN :caseIds
            AND IsClosed = true
            AND ContactId != null
        ];

        if (closedCases.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        Date dueDate = Date.today().addDays(2);
        List<Task> surveyTasks = new List<Task>();

        for (Case c : closedCases) {
            surveyTasks.add(new Task(
                WhatId       = c.Id,
                WhoId        = c.ContactId,
                Subject      = 'CSAT survey: ' + c.CaseNumber,
                ActivityDate = dueDate,
                Status       = 'Not Started'
            ));
        }

        // Partial success: one bad Task record shouldn't prevent the others
        // from being created. Failures are logged for follow-up.
        List<Database.SaveResult> results = Database.insert(surveyTasks, false);

        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            Database.SaveResult sr = results[i];
            if (!sr.isSuccess()) {
                for (Database.Error err : sr.getErrors()) {
                    System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                        'CaseSurveyJob: failed to create survey Task for Case '
                        + closedCases[i].CaseNumber + ' - ' + err.getMessage());
                }
            }
        }

        // Hook point: vendor survey callout integration can be added here
        // once available, using the same closedCases list.
    }
}
```

**Heads‑up before the code:** `Messaging.sendEmail` is limited to **10 invocations per Apex transaction** (regardless of how many messages you pass in the list), while the list‑based call lets you send up to 10 messages *per invocation* and returns individual `Messaging.SendEmailResult` objects with per‑message success/error info (even when `allOrNone = false`). Since the job can pass up to 200 opportunities, calling `Messaging.sendEmail` once per opportunity (200 calls) will blow past that 10‑call governor limit and throw a `LimitException` partway through, losing the "keep going on failure" behavior you actually want.

The safer, equally failure‑tolerant pattern is to build the full list of `SingleEmailMessage` objects, then call `Messaging.sendEmail(messages, false)` **once** (or in batches of 10 if you ever exceed 10 recipients, by chunking). Each `SendEmailResult` tells you whether that particular message succeeded, so one bad address/owner doesn't block the others — without touching the per‑transaction invocation limit.

Since the task explicitly asks for the call inside the loop (one per opportunity), I've implemented it exactly as requested below, but added a defensive try/catch per iteration plus a comment flagging the limit risk, and I'd strongly recommend switching to the batched-list approach before this goes to production with the full 200-record payload.

