/**
 * Sends renewal-reminder emails to opportunity owners.
 *
 * NOTE: Messaging.sendEmail is limited to 10 invocations per Apex transaction.
 * Calling it once per opportunity (as implemented below, per the stated requirement)
 * means batches larger than 10 opportunities in a single transaction will exceed
 * that limit and throw a LimitException for the remaining records. If the scheduled
 * job can pass up to 200 opportunities, prefer building a List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage>
 * and calling Messaging.sendEmail(messages, false) once (chunked in groups of 10 if needed),
 * which still reports per-message success/failure via Messaging.SendEmailResult without
 * consuming 200 separate invocations.
 */
public with sharing class RenewalReminderService {

    public static Integer sendReminders(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Integer sentCount = 0;

        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return sentCount;
        }

        List<Opportunity> opportunities = [
            SELECT Id, Name, CloseDate, OwnerId
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE Id IN :opportunityIds
        ];

        for (Opportunity opp : opportunities) {
            if (opp.OwnerId == null) {
                continue;
            }

            Messaging.SingleEmailMessage email = new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage();
            email.setTargetObjectId(opp.OwnerId);
            email.setSaveAsActivity(false);
            email.setSubject('Renewal due: ' + opp.Name);

            String closeDateStr = opp.CloseDate != null
                ? opp.CloseDate.format()
                : 'an unspecified date';

            email.setPlainTextBody(
                'This is a reminder that the opportunity "' + opp.Name +
                '" has a renewal due with a close date of ' + closeDateStr +
                '. Please review and take any necessary action.'
            );

            try {
                Messaging.SendEmailResult[] results = Messaging.sendEmail(
                    new List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage>{ email },
                    false
                );

                if (results != null && !results.isEmpty() && results[0].isSuccess()) {
                    sentCount++;
                }
            } catch (Exception ex) {
                // Swallow so one failure doesn't stop reminders for the rest of the opportunities.
                System.debug(
                    LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                    'RenewalReminderService: failed to send reminder for Opportunity ' +
                    opp.Id + ' - ' + ex.getMessage()
                );
            }
        }

        return sentCount;
    }
}
```

**Important change to your approach:** Loading `[SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry FROM Contact]` with no `WHERE` clause will try to pull **all 2.3 million contacts** into a single query/collection on every trigger run — even when the MDM batch only updates 200 accounts. That will blow past heap limits (6–12 MB), the 50k query row limit, and will be dramatically slower than necessary. It's also not actually "needed" — you only ever touch contacts belonging to the accounts that changed.

The fix that keeps your "one query, no queries in loops" intent while being safe: still issue **exactly one SOQL query**, but filter it with `WHERE AccountId IN :changedAccountIds`, scoped to just the accounts in the current trigger batch (max 200 since Account batches are capped at 200, so at most 200 AccountIds in the bind). This is the standard bulk-safe pattern — one query per trigger invocation, scoped to trigger context, not to the whole org.

Everything else follows your spec exactly: only accounts whose `BillingCountry` changed are considered, and only contacts whose `MailingCountry` is blank or equals the account's *previous* `BillingCountry` get updated.

